(window.DVR_PAPERS=window.DVR_PAPERS||{})["cs-classes-2027-076"]={"v":1,"id":"cs-classes-2027-076","title":"DVRUO Classes CS Test Series | Mixed Subjects | Full Length Test 1","sourceTitle":"DVRUO Classes CS Test Series | Mixed Subjects | Full Length Test 1","branch":"COMPUTER SCIENCE ENGINEERING","code":"CS","provider":"DVRUO CLASSES","providerKey":"classes","series":"DVRUO Classes Test Series for GATE CSE 2027","seriesId":"cs-classes-2027","num":76,"category":"other","year":"","format":"B","durationSec":10800,"info":{"orgName":"DVRUO Classes","batchName":"DVRUO Classes Test Series for GATE CSE 2027","subjectName":"Mixed Subjects"},"source":"COMPUTER SCIENCE ENGINEERING/GO CLASSES/GO Classes Test Series for GATE CSE 2027/092_exam_1340_GO Classes CS Test Series Mixed Subjects Full Length Test 1.html","questions":[{"n":1,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Let $Z=3 X-Y-5$, where $X$ and $Y$ are independent random variables with $\\operatorname{Var}(X)=1$ and $\\operatorname{Var}(Y)=2$. What is $\\operatorname{Var}(Z)?$</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>$4$</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>$7$</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>$11$</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>None of the above.</p>"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.3333},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> C</p><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/405001\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":2,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Suppose a digital communications channel has a probability of bit error of $p$, where all errors are independent of each other. If you transmit $N$ bits, what's the probability that exactly $k$ bits are received incorrectly?</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>$\\left(\\begin{array}{c}N \\\\ N-k\\end{array}\\right) p^{N-k}(1-p)^k$</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>$p^{k-1}(1-p)$</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>$(1-p)^{k-1} p$</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>$\\left(\\begin{array}{c}N \\\\ k\\end{array}\\right) p^k(1-p)^{N-k}$</p>"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.3333},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> D</p><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/404999\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":3,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>You ask your neighbor to water a sickly plant while you are on vacation. Without water, the plant will die with a probability $0.8.$ With water, it will die with a probability $0.15.$ You are $90$ percent certain the neighbor will remember to water the plant.<br>\nWhat is the probability that the plant will be alive when you return?</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>$0.85$</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>$0.79$</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>$0.90$</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>$0.63$</p>"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.3333},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> B</p><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/404997\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":4,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Given two Boolean random variables, $A$ and $B$, where $P(A)=1 / 2, \\quad P(B)=1 / 3,$&nbsp;and $\\mathrm{P}(A \\mid \\neg B)=1 / 4$, what is $\\mathrm{P}(A \\mid B)?$</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>$1 / 6$</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>$1 / 4$</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>$3 / 4$</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>$1$</p>"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.3333},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> D</p><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/404995\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":5,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Let $\\mathrm{A}, \\mathrm{B}$ denote events in a sample space $\\mathrm{S}$ and let $\\mathrm{P}(.)$ denote a probability measure on $\\mathrm{S}$. Which of the following is/are TRUE?</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>If $P(A \\mid B)=P(A)$ then $P(B \\mid A)=P(B)$.</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>If $P(A) \\leq P(B)$ then $A \\subset B$.</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>If $A \\subset B$ then $P(A) \\leq P(B)$</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>$P\\left(A \\mid B^{\\prime}\\right)=1-P(A \\mid B)$</p>"}],"answer":["A","C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A, C","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> A, C</p><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/404994\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":6,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"Consider now a refined probabilistic model with $n=2$ questions. For $i \\in\\{1,2\\}$, let $X_i$ be an indicator random variable which is $1$ iff question $i$ is answered correctly by the student. We know $\\mathbf{P}\\left[X_1=1 \\mid X_2=0\\right]=0.4, \\mathbf{P}\\left[X_2=1 \\mid X_1=1\\right]=0.8$ and $\\mathbf{P}\\left[X_1=1\\right]=0.5$\n\nCompute the $P\\left[X_2=1 \\mid X_1=0\\right]$","paragraph":null,"options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[0.7,0.7],"answerText":"0.7","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> 7</p><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/404992\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":7,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Let $\\mathrm{AX}=0$ be a system of linear equations, where $\\mathrm{A}=\\left[a_{i j}\\right]_{m \\times n}, \\mathrm{X}$ is a column matrix and 0 is a null matrix. If $\\operatorname{Rank}(A)=r$</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>Exactly $r$ linearly independent solutions</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>Exactly n linearly independent solutions</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>Exactly $n-r$ linearly independent solutions</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>None of these</p>"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.3333},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> C</p><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/404991\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":8,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Let $\\mathbf{x}=\\left[\\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 5\\end{array}\\right]$ and $\\mathbf{v}=\\left[\\begin{array}{c}-7 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{array}\\right]$. Which of the following statements must be true?</p>\n\n<ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-roman\">\n\t<li>$A=\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc}-7 &amp; 0 &amp; 0 \\\\ 4 &amp; 0 &amp; 0 \\\\ 2 &amp; 0 &amp; 0\\end{array}\\right]$ is of rank 1 such that $A \\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{v}$.</li>\n\t<li>$A=\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc}-7 &amp; 0 &amp; 0 \\\\ 0 &amp; -2 &amp; 0 \\\\ 0 &amp; -1 &amp; 0\\end{array}\\right]$ is of rank 2 such that $A \\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{v}$.</li>\n\t<li>$A=\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc}-7 &amp; 0 &amp; 0 \\\\ 0 &amp; -2 &amp; 0 \\\\ 0 &amp; 0 &amp; 2 / 5\\end{array}\\right]$ is of rank 3 such that $A \\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{v}$.</li>\n</ol>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>Only I</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>Only II</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>I, II</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>All of them</p>"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.3333},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> D</p><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/404990\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":9,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Suppose that $A$ is $n \\times m$. Which of the following statements must be true?</p><ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-roman\"><li>If $\\operatorname{Rank}(A)=n$ and $m&gt;n$ (i.e., $m \\geq n+1$ ), then the system $A \\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ has infinitely many solutions.</li><li>If $\\operatorname{Rank}(A)=m$ and $m \\leq n$, then $A \\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ has a unique solution.</li><li>If $\\operatorname{Rank}(A)=m$ and $m \\leq n$, then $A \\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ has at most one solution.</li><li>If $\\operatorname{Rank}(A)=n$ and $m&gt;n$, (i.e., $m \\geq n+1$ ), then $A \\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ has infinitely many solution.</li></ol>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>I, II, III</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>II, IV</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>I, III, IV</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>I, II, IV</p>"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.3333},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> C</p><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/404989\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":10,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Find an equation relating $a, b$ and $c$ so that the linear system<br>\n$$<br>\n\\begin{aligned}<br>\nx+2 y-3 z &amp; =a \\\\<br>\nx-y+8 z &amp; =b \\\\<br>\n-6 x-3 y-15 z &amp; =c<br>\n\\end{aligned}<br>\n$$<br>\nis consistent for any values of $a, b, c$ satisfying that equation.</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>$3 a-3 b+c=0$</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>$3 a+3 b+c=0$</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>$3 a+3 b-c=0$</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>$6 a-3 b-c=0$</p>"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.3333},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> B</p><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/404988\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":11,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"For what value of $c$ are the vectors $\\left(\\begin{array}{c}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{array}\\right),\\left(\\begin{array}{l}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{array}\\right)$ and $\\left(\\begin{array}{l}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ c\\end{array}\\right)$ in $\\mathbb{R}^3$ linearly dependent?","paragraph":null,"options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[1,1],"answerText":"1","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> 1</p><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/404986\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":12,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Consider the following augmented matrix where $\\ast$ can be any value that is not $0$ and not $1:$<br>\n$$<br>\n\\left[\\begin{array}{llll|l}<br>\n1 &amp; \\ast &amp; \\ast &amp; \\ast &amp; \\ast \\\\<br>\n0 &amp; 0 &amp; \\ast &amp; \\ast &amp; \\ast \\\\<br>\n0 &amp; 0 &amp; \\ast &amp; \\ast &amp; \\ast \\\\<br>\n0 &amp; 0 &amp; 1 &amp; \\ast &amp; \\ast<br>\n\\end{array}\\right]<br>\n$$<br>\nThe system of linear equations represented:</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>has a unique solution</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>has no unique solution</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>has infinite solutions</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>has no solutions</p>"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.3333},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> B</p><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/404984\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":13,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>A matrix $A$ can be row reduced to the following echelon form<br>\n$$<br>\n\\left[\\begin{array}{lllll}<br>\n1 &amp; 1 &amp; 3 &amp; 2 &amp; 1 \\\\<br>\n0 &amp; 1 &amp; 2 &amp; 1 &amp; 3 \\\\<br>\n0 &amp; 0 &amp; 0 &amp; a &amp; b \\\\<br>\n0 &amp; 0 &amp; 0 &amp; 0 &amp; c<br>\n\\end{array}\\right]<br>\n$$<br>\nDepending on the values of the constants $a, b$ and $c$ the rank of $A$</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>must be $2$</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>must be $3$</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>must be $4$</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>can be $2, 3 \\;\\text{or}\\; 4$</p>"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.3333},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> D</p><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/404983\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":14,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>For a connected, undirected graph $\\text{G = (V, E)},$ which of the following must be true?</p>\n\n<ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-roman\">\n\t<li>$\\sum_{v \\in \\text{V}}$ degree $(\\mathrm{v})$ is even.</li>\n\t<li>$|\\mathrm{E}| \\geq|\\mathrm{V}|-1$</li>\n\t<li>$\\mathrm{G}$ has at least one vertex with degree $1.$</li>\n</ol>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>I only</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>I, II only</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>I, III only</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>I, II, III</p>"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.3333},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> B</p><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/386399\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":15,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>An Euler circuit of an undirected graph is a circuit in which each edge of the graph appears exactly once. Which of the following undirected graphs must have an Euler circuit?</p>\n\n<ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-roman\">\n\t<li>A complete graph with $12$ vertices</li>\n\t<li>A complete graph with $13$ vertices</li>\n\t<li>A tree with $13$ vertices</li>\n</ol>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>I only</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>II only</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>III only</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>I and II Only</p>"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.3333},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> B</p><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/386397\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":16,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>A full binary tree is a rooted tree in which every internal node has exactly two children. How many internal nodes are there in a full binary tree with $500$ leaves?</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>$250$</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>$499$</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>$500$</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>$501$</p>"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.3333},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> B</p><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/386395\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":17,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"Let $\\mathrm{G}$ be a non-cyclic group of order $57 .$ Then the number of elements of order $5$ in $\\mathrm{G}$ is _______","paragraph":null,"options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[0,0],"answerText":"0","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Not available</p><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/386391\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":18,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"The number of different Total order relations on a set of $7$ elements is _______","paragraph":null,"options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[5040,5040],"answerText":"5040","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Not available</p><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/386389\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":19,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Consider a $5 \\times 6$ rectangular grid as depicted in Figure $1.$ A staircase walk is a path in the grid which moves only right or up.</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align:center\"><img alt=\"\" height=\"249\" src=\"media/4a/4a5df325bec6a8274dae.png\" width=\"589\"></p>\n\n<p>What is the total number of staircase walks from $\\text{A}$ to $\\text{B}$ that may or may not pass through the point $\\text{P?}$</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[462,462],"answerText":"462","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Not available</p><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/386387\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":20,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Consider the statement form $\\mathrm{S}:$<br>\n$$<br>\np \\vee(\\sim p \\wedge q) \\Rightarrow \\sim q .<br>\n$$<br>\nWhich of the following is true?</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>$\\text{S}$ is a tautology</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>$\\text{S}$ is a Contradiction</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>$\\text{S}$ is a Contingency</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>Antecedent of $\\text{S}$ is equivalent to consequent of $\\text{S}$</p>"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.3333},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> C</p><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/386385\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":21,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"What is the order of smallest non-cyclic group?","paragraph":null,"options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[4,4],"answerText":"4","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> 4</p><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/386383\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":22,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Let $\\mathcal{P}(A)$ denote the power set of $\\text{A}.$ If $\\mathcal{P}(\\text{A}) \\subseteq \\text{B}$ then</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>$2^{|\\text{A}|} \\leq|\\text{B}|$</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>$2^{|\\text{A}|} \\geq|\\text{B}|$</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>$2|\\text{A}|&lt;|\\text{B}|$</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>$|\\text{A}|+2 \\leq|\\text{B}|$</p>"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.3333},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> A</p><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/386382\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":23,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"The number of partitions of the set $\\text{X} = \\{a, b, c, d\\}$ with “$a$” and “$b$” in the same block is ________","paragraph":null,"options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[5,5],"answerText":"5","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> 5</p><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/386380\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":24,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Given a graph $\\mathrm{G}$ is a connected planar simple graph with ' $e$ ' edges and ' $v$ ' vertices, with $v \\geq 3$ and if all cycles in $\\text{G}$ have length $\\geq 4$, then which of the following relations hold always?<br>\n&nbsp;</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>$e \\leq 2 v-4$</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>$e \\leq 2 v-2$</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>$e \\leq 3 v-4$</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>None of these</p>"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.3333},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> A</p><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/386378\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":25,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Which of the following statements is TRUE?</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>For all sets $\\text{A, B}, $ and $\\text{C, A - (B - C) = (A - B) - C}$.</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>For all sets $\\text{A, B}, $ and $\\text{C, (A - B)} \\cap \\text{(C - B) = (A} \\cap \\text{C)- B}$.</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>For all sets $\\text{A, B}, $ and $\\text{C, (A - B)} \\cap \\text{(C - B) = A - (B} \\cup \\text{C)}$.</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>For all sets $\\text{A, B}, $ and $\\text{C},$ if $\\text{A} \\cap \\text{C = B} \\cap \\text{C}$ then $\\text{A = B}$.</p>"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.3333},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> B</p><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/386377\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":26,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>To show that the circuit corresponding to the Boolean expression $(\\text{P} \\wedge \\text{Q}) \\vee(\\sim P \\wedge \\text{Q}) \\vee$ $(\\sim \\text{P}\\wedge \\sim \\text{Q})$ can be represented using two logical gates, one shows that this Boolean expression is equal to $\\sim \\text{P}\\vee&nbsp;\\text{Q}$. The circuit corresponding to $(\\text{P} \\wedge \\text{Q} \\wedge \\text{R}) \\vee(\\sim \\text{P}\\wedge \\text{Q} \\wedge$ $\\text{R}) \\vee(\\sim \\text{P}\\wedge(\\sim \\text{Q} \\vee \\sim \\text{R})$ computes the same function as the circuit corresponding to</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>$(\\text{P} \\wedge \\text{Q}) \\vee \\sim \\text{R}$</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>$\\text{P} \\vee(\\text{Q} \\wedge \\text{R})$</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>$\\sim \\text{P}\\vee(\\text{Q} \\wedge \\text{R})$</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>$(\\text{P} \\wedge \\sim \\text{Q}) \\vee \\text{R}$</p>"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.3333},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> C</p><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/386376\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":27,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>The power set $\\mathcal{P}((\\text{A} \\times \\text{B}) \\cup(B \\times \\text{A}))$ has the same number of elements as the power set $\\mathcal{P}((\\text{A} \\times \\text{B}) \\cup(\\text{A} \\times \\text{B}))$ if and only if</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>$\\text{A = B}$</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>$\\text{A}=\\emptyset$ or $\\text{B}=\\emptyset$</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>$\\text{B}=\\emptyset$ or $\\text{A = B}$</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>$\\text{A} = \\emptyset$ or $\\text{B} = \\emptyset$ or $\\text{A = B}$</p>"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.3333},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> D</p><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/386375\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":28,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"Number of Linearly independent eigenvectors corresponding to the matrix\n$$\n\\left(\\begin{array}{ccc}\n1 & 0 & 0 \\\\\n-2 & 2 & -1 \\\\\n4 & 0 & 3 \\\\\n\\end{array}\\right)\n$$","paragraph":null,"options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[3,3],"answerText":"3","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> 3</p><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/386366\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":29,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Which of the following formulas is a formalization of the sentence: \"Every connected and circuit-free graph is a tree\". Where cir( $\\mathrm{x})$ means \" $\\mathrm{x}$ has circuit\"</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>$\\forall \\mathrm{x}[(\\operatorname{conn}(\\mathrm{x}) \\wedge \\neg \\operatorname{cir}(\\mathrm{x})) \\rightarrow \\operatorname{tree}(\\mathrm{x})]$</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>$\\neg \\exists x[(\\operatorname{conn}(\\mathrm{x}) \\wedge \\neg \\operatorname{cir}(\\mathrm{x})) \\wedge \\neg \\operatorname{tree}(\\mathrm{x})]$</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>$\\forall \\mathrm{x}[(\\operatorname{conn}(\\mathrm{x}) \\wedge \\neg \\operatorname{cir}(\\mathrm{x})) \\wedge \\operatorname{tree}(\\mathrm{x})]$</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>$\\forall \\mathrm{x}[(\\operatorname{conn}(\\mathrm{x}) \\wedge \\operatorname{cir}(\\mathrm{x})) \\rightarrow \\operatorname{tree}(\\mathrm{x})]$</p>"}],"answer":["A","B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A, B","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> A, B</p><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/386350\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":30,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Let $[P(S), \\supseteq]$ be a partial order relation defined on $P(S),$ where $S = \\{1,2,3,4\\}. P(S)$ is the set of all subsets of $S.$ Which of the following is the join of $\\{1,2\\}, \\{3,4\\}?$</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>$\\{3,4\\}$</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>$\\{1,2,3,4\\}$</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>$\\varnothing $</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>$\\{1,2\\}$</p>"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.3333},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> C</p><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/375372\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":31,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Consider the matrix $\\mathbf{A} \\in \\mathbb{R}^{2 \\times 2}$, which takes a vector $\\vec{v}=\\left[\\begin{array}{l}x \\\\ y\\end{array}\\right]$ and scales its $y$ component by $2,$ but leaves the $x$ component unchanged. In other words, $\\mathbf{A}\\left[\\begin{array}{l}x \\\\ y\\end{array}\\right]=\\left[\\begin{array}{c}x \\\\ 2 y\\end{array}\\right]$.</p>\n\n<p>What are the eigenvalues of $\\mathbf{A}?$</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>$1,0$</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>$2,0$</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>$1,2$</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>$-1,2$</p>"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.6667},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> C</p><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/404981\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":32,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Suppose $A=\\left[\\begin{array}{llll}\\mathbf{a}_1 &amp; \\mathbf{a}_2 &amp; \\cdots &amp; \\mathbf{a}_n\\end{array}\\right]$ is an $n \\times n$ invertible matrix, and $\\mathbf{b}$ is a non-zero vector in $\\mathbb{R}^n$. Which of the following statements is / are true?</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>$\\mathbf{b}$ is a linear combination of $\\left\\{\\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2, \\ldots, \\mathbf{a}_n\\right\\}$.</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>The determinant of $A$ is nonzero.</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>$\\operatorname{rank} A=n$.</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>If $A \\mathbf{b}=\\lambda \\mathbf{b}$ for some constant $\\lambda$, then $\\lambda \\neq 0$.</p>"}],"answer":["A","B","C","D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A, B, C, D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> A, B, C, D</p><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/404980\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":33,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Consider the matrix $A=\\left[\\begin{array}{rrrr}1 &amp; 3 &amp; 1 &amp; 0 \\\\ 0 &amp; 2 &amp; 4 &amp; -2\\end{array}\\right]$ whose row echelon form is $\\left[\\begin{array}{llll}1 &amp; 0 &amp; a &amp; b \\\\ 0 &amp; 1 &amp; c &amp; d\\end{array}\\right]$. Find $a+b+c+d$ :</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>$-7$</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>$-4$</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>$-3$</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>$-1$</p>"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.6667},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> D</p><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/404978\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":34,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Suppose you wish to determine whether a set of vectors $\\left\\{\\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2, \\mathbf{v}_3, \\mathbf{v}_4\\right\\}$ is linearly independent. You form the matrix $A=\\left[\\begin{array}{llll}\\mathbf{v}_1 &amp; \\mathbf{v}_2 &amp; \\mathbf{v}_3 &amp; \\mathbf{v}_4\\end{array}\\right]$ and you calculate its reduced row echelon form, $R=\\left[\\begin{array}{llll}1 &amp; 0 &amp; 2 &amp; 1 \\\\ 0 &amp; 1 &amp; 3 &amp; 1 \\\\ 0 &amp; 0 &amp; 0 &amp; 0\\end{array}\\right]$. You now decide to write $\\mathbf{v}_2$ as a linear combination of $\\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_3$, and $\\mathbf{v}_4$. Which is /are correct linear combination?</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>$\\mathbf{v}_2=\\mathbf{v}_3-2 \\mathbf{v}_4$</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>$\\mathbf{v}_2=-3 \\mathbf{v}_3-\\mathbf{v}_4$</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>$\\mathbf{v}_2=\\mathbf{v}_4-3 \\mathbf{v}_3$</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>$\\mathbf{v}_2=-\\mathbf{v}_1+\\mathbf{v}_4$</p>"}],"answer":["A","D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A, D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> A, D</p><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/404977\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":35,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>A $5$-bit codeword is sent over a noisy channel, where bits are flipped with probability $p$, each independently. An error-correcting code is designed such that if two or fewer bits of a codeword are flipped, the codeword can be correctly decode.</p>\n\n<p>What's the probability a codeword cannot be decoded?</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>$\\left(\\begin{array}{l}5 \\\\ 2\\end{array}\\right) p^2(1-p)^3$</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>$p^5(1-p)$</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>$\\left(\\begin{array}{l}5 \\\\ 3\\end{array}\\right) p^3(1-p)^2+5 p^4(1-p)+p^5$</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>$\\left(\\begin{array}{l}5 \\\\ 3\\end{array}\\right) p^3(1-p)^2$</p>"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.6667},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> C</p><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/404975\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":36,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Let $X$ be a random variable that takes values from $0$ to $9$ with equal probability $1 / 10$.</p>\n\n<p>Let $Z$ be a random variable $Z=(5 \\bmod (X+1))$.</p>\n\n<p>Find $P(Z=1)?$</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>$1 / 10$</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>$2 / 10$</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>$5 / 10$</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>$0$</p>"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.6667},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> B</p><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/404973\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":37,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"Let $A$ and $B$ are two events not necessarily independent.\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n& P(A, B)=0.15 \\\\\n& P\\left(A^c, B^c\\right)=0.45 \\\\\n& P(A)=0.6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nWhat is the probability of $P\\left(A^c, B\\right)?$","paragraph":null,"options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[0.1,0.1],"answerText":"0.1","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> 1</p><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/404971\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":38,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Consider the following scenario:<br>\nA box contains three coins: two regular coins and one fake two-headed coin, which always lands heads up.<br>\nIf you randomly pick a coin from the box and toss it, what is the probability that it lands heads up?</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>$1 / 2$</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>$2 / 3$</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>$1 / 3$</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>$3 / 4$</p>"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.6667},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> B</p><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/404969\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":39,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Anne and Betty go fishing. Assume they catch fish independently and that each has a probability $0 &lt; p &lt; 1$&nbsp;of catching no fish. Let $A$ be the event that Anne catches no fish and $B$ be the event that Betty catches no fish.&nbsp;</p>\n\n<p>Find the conditional probability that Anne catches no fish given that at least one of them catches no fish i.e. $P(A \\mid(A \\cup B))$.</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>$1 /(1-p)$</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>$1 /(2-p)$</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>$1 / p$</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>None of these</p>"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.6667},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> B</p><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/404967\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":40,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>We have a circuit with two devices between points $a$ and $b$. The circuit will work if there is at least one path between $a$ and $b$. The probability of each device working is shown on the graph, and we assume independent device failures. What is the probability of the circuit functioning correctly?</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align:center\"><img alt=\"\" height=\"173\" src=\"media/6e/6e8c84226ca6b1330879.png\" width=\"400\"></p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>$0.905$</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>$0.998$</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>$0.915$</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>$0.940$</p>"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.6667},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> B</p><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/404965\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":41,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Let $\\mathrm{G}$ be a finite group. Let \"$1$\" denote the identity element of $\\text{G}.$ If $g$ is an element of a finite group $\\mathrm{G},$&nbsp;then the order of $g$ is the smallest positive integer $n$ such that $g^ n=1$, and it is denoted $o(g)=n$. The order of group $\\mathrm{G}$ is denoted by $o(\\mathrm{G})$ or $|\\mathrm{G}|.$</p>\n\n<p>Which of the following statements is necessarily true?</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>If $g^ m=1,$&nbsp;for some $g \\in \\mathrm{G},$&nbsp;then $o(g) \\mid m$.</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>If $g^ m=1,$&nbsp;for some $g \\in \\mathrm{G},$&nbsp;then $o(g)=m$.</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>If $g^ m=1,$&nbsp;for some $\\mathrm{g} \\in \\mathrm{G},$&nbsp;then $m \\mid o(\\mathrm{g})$.</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>If $g^ m=1,$&nbsp;for some $g \\in \\mathrm{G},$&nbsp;then $m \\mid o(\\mathrm{G})$.</p>"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.6667},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> A</p><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/404963\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":42,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>If $\\text{G}$ is a group and $g \\in \\text{G}$, then the subgroup generated by $g$ is<br>\n$$<br>\n\\langle g\\rangle=\\left\\{g^n \\mid n \\in \\mathbb{Z}\\right\\}<br>\n$$<br>\nThe set of all positive integers less than $\\mathrm{n}$ and relatively prime to $\\mathrm{n}$ with multiplication $\\bmod \\mathrm{n}$ is a group denoted $\\text{U}_n$.<br>\ni.e. $\\text{U}_n =\\left\\{g \\in Z_{-} n \\mid \\operatorname{gcd}(g, n)=1\\right\\}$.</p>\n\n<p>Which of the following is/are true about $\\text{U}_{20}?$</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>In $\\text{U}_{20},$ the size of the subgroup generated by $3$ is $6.$</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>$\\text{U}_{20}$ is an abelian group.</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>$\\text{U}_{20}$ is a cyclic group.</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>The order of an element $y$ in a group $\\text{G}$ is equal to the size of the subgroup generated by $y$.</p>"}],"answer":["B","D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B, D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> B, D</p><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/404961\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":43,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Let $\\mathrm{G}$ be a group and $g \\in \\mathrm{G}$. Let \"$1$\" denote the identity element of $\\text{G}.$ The order of $g \\in \\mathrm{G}$ is denoted $\\mathrm{o}(\\mathrm{g})$. The order of group $\\mathrm{G}$ is denoted by $\\mathrm{o}(\\mathrm{G})$ or $|\\mathrm{G}|$.</p>\n\n<p>Which of the following statements is/are true?</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>$g^2=1$ if and only if $o(g)=1$ or&nbsp;$2.$</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>$o(g)=o\\left(g^{-1}\\right)$.</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>If $o(g)=n$ for some $n \\in \\text{Z}^+,$&nbsp;then $g^{(-1)}=g^{(n-1)}$.</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>$o(g)=o\\left(h g h^{-1}\\right)$ for any $h \\in \\text{G}$.</p>"}],"answer":["A","B","C","D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A, B, C, D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> A, B, C, D</p><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/404959\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":44,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Let $\\text{G}$ be a group. Let \"$1$\" denote the identity element of $\\text{G}.$ Which of the following is/are true?</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>if $a^{-1}=a$ for all $a \\in \\text{G}$, then $\\text{G}$ is abelian.</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>if for all $a, b, c \\in \\text{G}, a b=c a \\Rightarrow b=c$, then $\\text{G}$ is abelian</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>If $(a * b)^{-1}=\\left(b^{-1}\\right) *\\left(a^{-1}\\right)$ for every $a, b \\in \\text{G},$,= then $\\mathrm{G}$ is abelian.</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>$(a b)^n=a^n b^n$ for all $a, b \\in \\text{G}$, $n \\in Z$ if and only if $\\text{G}$ is abelian.</p>"}],"answer":["A","B","D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A, B, D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> A, B, D</p><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/404957\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":45,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Let $\\text{G}=\\langle\\{e, a, b, c, d\\}, *\\rangle$ be a group with the following Cayley table partially completed.<br>\n$$\\begin{array}{|l|l|l|l|l|l|}<br>\n\\hline&nbsp;\\ast&nbsp;&amp; e &amp; a &amp; b &amp; c &amp; d \\\\<br>\n\\hline e &amp; e &amp; a &amp; b &amp; c &amp; d \\\\<br>\n\\hline a &amp; a &amp; &amp; e &amp; b &amp; \\\\<br>\n\\hline b &amp; b &amp; &amp; &amp; &amp; \\\\<br>\n\\hline c &amp; c &amp; &amp; &amp; &amp; \\\\<br>\n\\hline d &amp; d &amp; &amp; &amp; &amp; \\\\<br>\n\\hline<br>\n\\end{array}$$<br>\nWhich of the following is/are true for the given group $\\text{G}?$</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>$\\text{G}$ is a cyclic group.</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>$\\mathrm{G}$ is abelian.</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>The number of generators in $\\mathrm{G}$ is $4.$</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>The order of $d$ is $2.$</p>"}],"answer":["A","B","C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A, B, C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> A, B, C</p><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/404955\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":46,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>A $k$-ary tree is a tree in which every node has at most $k$ children. In a $k$-ary tree with $n$ nodes and height $h,$&nbsp;which of the following is an upper bound for the maximum number of leaves as a function of $h, k$, and $n?$ (height of the tree is the number of edges in the longest path from root to some leaf)</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>$\\log_k n$</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>$n / \\log _{k}(n)$</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>$k^h$</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>$h^k$</p>"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.6667},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> C</p><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/386401\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":47,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"A graph is said to be $2$-colourable if each vertex can be coloured either red or blue and no two vertices of the same colour are connected by an edge. If some graph is not $2$-colorable, then we can reduce it to become $2$-colorable by deleting some edges. We are given any simple graph with $101$ nodes. $k$ is the least required number of edges we have to delete in order to make this graph $2$-colorable (Eg: $k=0$ for a graph which is already $2$-colorable).\nThe minimum value for ' $k$ ' to reach the worst case is _______","paragraph":null,"options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[2500,2500],"answerText":"2500","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Not available</p><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/386352\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":48,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Which of the following statements is/are True?</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>$\\{2,3,4\\} \\subseteq \\text{A}$ implies that $2 \\in \\text{A}$ and $\\{3,4\\} \\subseteq \\text{A}.$</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>$\\{2,3,4\\} \\in \\text{A}$ and $\\{2,3\\} \\in \\text{B}$ implies that $\\{4\\} \\subseteq \\text{A - B}.$</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>$\\text{A} \\cap \\text{B} \\supseteq\\{2,3,4\\}$ implies that $\\{2,3,4\\} \\subseteq \\text{A}$ and $\\{2,3,4\\} \\subseteq \\text{B}.$</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>$\\text{A - B} \\supseteq\\{3,4\\}$ and $\\{1,2\\} \\subseteq \\text{B}$ implies that $\\{1,2,3,4\\} \\subseteq \\text{A} \\cup \\text{B}.$</p>"}],"answer":["A","C","D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A, C, D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> A, C, D</p><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/386349\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":49,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Let $\\text{A}$ be any set.<br>\nThe following are the definitions of Irreflexive and Asymmetric Relations :</p>\n\n<ol start=\"1\" style=\"list-style-type:lower-alpha\">\n\t<li>A binary relation $\\text{R}$ on a set $\\mathcal{A}$ is irreflexive iff $(x, x) \\notin \\text{R}$ for all $x \\in \\mathcal{A}$.</li>\n\t<li>A binary relation $\\text{R}$ on a set $\\mathcal{A}$ is asymmetric iff whenever $(x, y) \\in \\text{R}$, then $(y, x) \\notin \\text{R}$.</li>\n</ol>\n\n<p>We define a \"strict order relation\" as follows :<br>\nA binary relation $\\text{R}$ on a set $\\text{A}$ is a strict order on $\\text{A}$ iff $\\text{R}$ is irreflexive and transitive.<br>\nWhich of the following is/are true?</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>Every strict order is asymmetric.</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>Every strict order is total order relation.</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>Every strict order is antisymmetric.</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>Every relation which is both Irreflexive and Asymmetric, is strict order.</p>"}],"answer":["A","C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A, C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> A, C</p><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/386341\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":50,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"Find the number of positive integers $n \\in N$, such that $1 \\leq n \\leq 1000$, which are not divisible by either $4,10$ or $15 .$","paragraph":null,"options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[667,667],"answerText":"667","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Not available</p><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/386337\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":51,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Let $n \\in \\mathrm{N}$,</p>\n\n<p>Consider the following two combinatorial identities :</p>\n\n<ol>\n\t<li>$\\left(\\begin{array}{c}2 n \\\\2\\\\\\end{array}\\right)=2\\left(\\begin{array}{l}n\\\\2\\\\\\end{array}\\right)+n^2 .$</li>\n\t<li>$\\left(\\begin{array}{l}n\\\\0\\\\\\end{array}\\right)+2^1\\left(\\begin{array}{l}n\\\\1\\\\\\end{array}\\right)+2^2\\left(\\begin{array}{l}n\\\\2\\\\\\end{array}\\right)+\\ldots+2^k\\left(\\begin{array}{l}n\\\\k\\\\\\end{array}\\right)+\\ldots+2^n\\left(\\begin{array}{l}n\\\\n\\\\\\end{array}\\right)=3^n .$</li>\n</ol>\n\n<p>Which of the above are correct?</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>Only $1$</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>Only $2$</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>Both</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>None</p>"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.6667},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> C</p><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/386335\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":52,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Which of the following propositional logic formulas is/are valid?</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>$(p \\rightarrow q) \\wedge \\neg q \\rightarrow \\neg p$</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>$(p \\rightarrow q) \\rightarrow(p \\rightarrow \\neg q)$</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>$(p \\vee q \\rightarrow \\mathrm{r}) \\vee p \\vee q$</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>$(p \\vee q) \\wedge(p \\rightarrow r \\wedge q) \\wedge(q \\rightarrow \\neg r \\wedge p)$</p>"}],"answer":["A","C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A, C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> A, C</p><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/386333\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":53,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Let $f: \\text{X} \\rightarrow \\text{Y}$ and $g: \\text{Y} \\rightarrow \\text{Z}.$ Let $h=g \\circ f: \\text{X} \\rightarrow \\text{Z}.$ Suppose $g$ is one-to-one and onto. Which of the following is FALSE?</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>If $f$ is one-to-one then $h$ is one-to-one and onto.</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>If $f$ is not onto then $h$ is not onto.</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>If $f$ is not one-to-one then $h$ is not one-to-one.</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>If $f$ is one-to-one then $h$ is one-to-one.</p>"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.6667},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> A</p><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/386326\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":54,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Suppose $\\text{Z}_1, \\text{Z}_2, \\text{Z}_3$ are independent Bernoulli random variables with parameter $p=\\dfrac{1}{2}$.<br>\nThe random variable is defined as follows:<br>\n$$<br>\n\\text{S}= \\begin{cases}\\text{Z}_2 \\text{Z}_3 &amp; \\text { if } \\text{Z}_1=1,\\\\<br>\n\\text{Z}_2+\\text{Z}_3 &amp; \\text { if } \\text{Z}_1=0 .\\end{cases}<br>\n$$<br>\nFind $\\text{P(S}=1)$.</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>$3 / 8$</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>$1 / 2$</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>$1 / 8$</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>None of these</p>"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.6667},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> A</p><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/386315\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":55,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Suppose $\\text{Y}$ has a discrete distribution with pmf given by<br>\n$$<br>\n\\begin{array}{|c|c|c|c|c|}<br>\n\\hline y &amp; 0 &amp; 1 &amp; 2 &amp; 3\\\\<br>\n\\hline p_\\textsf{Y}(y) &amp; 1 / 2 &amp; 1 / 4 &amp; 1 / 8 &amp; 1 / 8\\\\<br>\n\\hline<br>\n\\end{array}$$<br>\nCalculate $\\text{E}\\left[(\\text{Y}-1)^2\\right]?$</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>$1 / 4$</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>$1 / 8$</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>$9 / 8$</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>$3 / 4$</p>"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.6667},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> C</p><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/386307\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":56,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>A parallel system functions whenever at least one of its components works. Consider a parallel system of $n$ components and suppose that each component works with probability $p$ independently.<br>\nWhat is the probability the system is functioning?</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>$(1-p)^n$</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>$p^n$</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>$1-(1-p)^n$</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>$1-p^n$</p>"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.6667},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> C</p><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/386301\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":57,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Let $\\text{A}$ be an $n \\times n$ matrix. Its only eigenvalues are $1,2,3,4,5$, possibly with multiplicities.</p>\n\n<p>What is the nullity of the matrix $\\text{A + I}_n,$ where $\\text{I}_n$ is the $n \\times n$ identity matrix?</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>$0$</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>$1$</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>$n-1$</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>$n-5$</p>"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.6667},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> A</p><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/386297\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":58,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Which of the following statements is true for every matrix $\\text{M}$ with $m$ rows and $n$ columns, where $m&lt;n?$</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>The system $\\text{M} \\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ has no solution.</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>The system $\\text{M} \\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ has infinitely many solutions.</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>For every $\\mathbf{b}$ in $\\mathbb{R}^m$, the system $\\text{M} \\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ has infinitely many solutions.</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>For some $\\mathbf{b}$ in $\\mathbb{R}^m$, the system $\\text{M} \\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ has a unique solution.</p>"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.6667},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> B</p><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/386294\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":59,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Let $\\text{A}=\\left[\\begin{array}{cc}<br>\n1 &amp; -1 \\\\<br>\n0 &amp; 2<br>\n\\end{array}\\right]$, and let $\\text{B}$ be a $2 \\times 3$ matrix. Suppose that the second row of $\\text{(A B)}^\\text{T}$ is $\\mathbf{v}=[-1 \\quad 3]$<br>\nCompute the second column of $\\text{B}$.</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>$<br>\n\t\\left[\\begin{array}{c}<br>\n\t-1\\\\<br>\n\t3\\\\<br>\n\t\\end{array}\\right]<br>\n\t$</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>$<br>\n\t\\left[\\begin{array}{l}<br>\n\t\\frac{1}{2}\\\\<br>\n\t\\frac{3}{2}\\\\<br>\n\t\\end{array}\\right]<br>\n\t$</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>$<br>\n\t\\left[\\begin{array}{l}<br>\n\t\\frac{1}{3}\\\\<br>\n\t1\\\\<br>\n\t\\end{array}\\right]<br>\n\t$</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>$<br>\n\t\\left[\\begin{array}{c}<br>\n\t3\\\\<br>\n\t-1\\\\<br>\n\t\\end{array}\\right]<br>\n\t$</p>"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.6667},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> B</p><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/386290\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":60,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"The set $\\{1, 2, 3, \\dots , n - 1\\}$ is a group under multiplication modulo $n.$ Then the smallest value of $n$ between $20$ and $30$ is $(20, 30$ included $)\\;$ _______","paragraph":null,"options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[23,23],"answerText":"23","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Not available</p><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/376095\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":61,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Which of the following functions are one-to-one but not onto.</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>$f: \\mathbb{R} \\rightarrow \\mathbb{R}$ defined by $f(x)=e^{x}$</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>$f: \\mathbb{Z} \\rightarrow \\mathbb{Z}$ defined by $f(n)=n^{2}+3$</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>$f: \\mathbb{R} \\rightarrow \\mathbb{R}$ defined by $f(x)=\\sin x$</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>$f: \\mathbb{Z} \\rightarrow \\mathbb{Z}$ defined by $f(x)=x^{2}$</p>"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> A</p><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/376077\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":62,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Assume the specifications</p>\n\n<ul>\n\t<li>$F(x,y) : x$ is the father of $y$</li>\n\t<li>$M(x,y) : x$ is the mother of $y.$</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p>Which of the formulas in predicate logic below express the sentence 'Everybody has a mother'?</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>$\\exists x \\forall y M(x, y)$</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>$\\exists y \\forall x M(x, y)$</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>$\\forall x \\exists y M(x, y)$</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>$\\forall y \\exists x M(x, y)$</p>"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> D</p><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/375374\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":63,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Let $(L, \\#)$ be a lattice, where $\\#$ is some partial order relation defined on the base set $L$. Let $x,y,z \\in L.$ Which of the following is/are necessarily true?</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>$x \\vee y = y \\vee x$</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>$x \\vee (x \\wedge y) = x$</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>$x \\vee (y \\wedge z) = (x \\vee y) \\wedge (x \\vee z)$</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>$x \\wedge (x \\vee y) = x$</p>"}],"answer":["A","B","D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A, B, D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> A, B, D</p><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/375371\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":64,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p><strong><img alt=\"\" height=\"281\" src=\"media/c1/c1b49a376a74c8d5b322.jpg\" width=\"312\"></strong></p>\n\n<p>The number&nbsp;of elements which do not have complements in the above lattices is __________</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[6,6],"answerText":"6","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> 6</p><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/375368\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":65,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Let $A, B$ be two predicate logic formulas. We say that “$A \\models B$” (which means that “$A$ infers $B$”) iff for all interpretations in which $A$ is true, $B$ is also true.<br>\nSo, “$A \\models B$” iff “$A \\rightarrow B$” is valid.</p>\n\n<p>Which of the following inferences are valid in predicate logic?</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>$\\forall x (P(x) \\vee Q(x)) \\models \\forall x P(x) \\vee \\forall x Q(x)$</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>$\\forall x (P(x) \\rightarrow Q(x)) \\models \\forall x P(x) \\rightarrow \\forall x Q(x)$</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>$\\forall x P(x) \\rightarrow \\forall x Q(x) \\models \\forall x (P(x) \\rightarrow Q(x))$</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>$\\neg \\forall x (P(x) \\wedge Q(x)) \\models \\exists x \\neg P(x) \\wedge \\exists x \\neg Q(x)$</p>"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> B</p><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/375360\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"}],"sectionsMeta":[{"name":"Technical","marks":100.0,"instructions":null}],"math":true,"scoring":"marks","sections":[{"name":"Technical","start":0,"count":65}],"stats":{"questions":65,"maxMarks":100,"types":{"MCQ":37,"MSQ":15,"NAT":13},"withoutKey":0}};
